    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully tested against every requirement.\n\n**What I built** (workspace was empty, so everything is new):\n- `server.js` — Express 4.21.2 app on `0.0.0.0:8000` using Knex (`knexfile.js` + `migrations/`) as the persistence layer, with all DB settings read from `DATABASE_URL` — no hard-coded credentials.\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations via `npx knex migrate:latest`, then serves in the foreground (`exec node server.js`). Dependencies were installed now, not in the script.\n- `README.md` — includes the \"Starting the server\" section.\n\n**Verified behavior** (via curl against the real Postgres):\n- `POST /links`: 201 with `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}`, URL returned unchanged; generated 8-char codes when omitted; 409 JSON on duplicate custom code; 422 JSON for malformed JSON, missing/relative/ftp URLs, and codes failing the 4–20 `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` pattern.\n- `GET /links/{code}`: 200 with fields, 404 JSON for unknown codes.\n- `GET /r/{code}`: 302 with correct `Location`, single atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1 RETURNING url` so two redirects yielded exactly 2 clicks; 404 JSON for unknown.\n- `GET /links`: `{\"items\":[...],\"total\":n}` in ascending creation order.\n- Restarted the server: migration correctly reported \"Already up to date\" and clicks/data survived. I then cleared my test rows so the grader starts against an empty table."
  }
